WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.150
(upbeat music)
(audience clapping)

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:05.790
- Do you mind if I tag into
the journaling conversation

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:07.380
you're having?
- Yes. Do you journal?

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:08.213
- I do.

00:00:08.213 --> 00:00:11.190
I say this and especially you
just played a clip from it,

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:13.710
but I find if you write down

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:15.750
every few nights what you're saddest about

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.670
or angriest about, you can start

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:19.350
to see your own life as a story.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.030
And when you see your own life as a story,

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.590
sometimes you can zoom out

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:25.380
and encourage the main character
to make better decisions.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:28.050
- Wow.
(audience clapping)

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.431
- I find, I always-

00:00:30.431 --> 00:00:31.360
- I love how you just worded that.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.390
Also, it's probably
great material for you.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:35.704
- Well, it's true.
- All your saddest stuff.

00:00:35.704 --> 00:00:37.920
- It's honestly like,
it's the basis of, like,

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.440
so much of "The Old Man
and the Pool" is like,

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.120
- Yeah.
- Finding the humor

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.850
in the things that are the hardest.

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:45.960
- Yeah.
- Because I feel

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.520
like that's sort of like, I don't know.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.860
I think that's the coping
mechanism of comedy.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:51.150
- Yeah. Which is a...

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:52.991
It's a good coping mechanism.

00:00:52.991 --> 00:00:54.060
- (chuckles) It's not bad.
- Yes.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:57.030
So, I heard that you
didn't initially want kids,

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:57.863
right, in life?

00:00:57.863 --> 00:00:59.253
I didn't either initially.

00:00:59.253 --> 00:01:00.086
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:01:00.086 --> 00:01:02.207
- I was reluctant my... (chuckles)

00:01:02.207 --> 00:01:03.040
- Yeah.
- I wrote,

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.980
my last special was called "The New One,"

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.430
and it was all about how I
never want to have a child

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:12.600
and all the reasons why
and how I had a child

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.410
and how I was right.
(all laughing)

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.960
And then, ultimately, how I was wrong.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.120
And that's why it's emotional.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.160
And, you know, these
shows I always try to,

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.130
I've done about five of these
solo shows and yeah, that's me

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:28.655
and my daughter at "The
Old Man and the Pool."

00:01:28.655 --> 00:01:29.490
- Oh, that's so cool.
- Yeah.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:31.350
That was at Lincoln Center.
- Look at you now.

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:32.190
- Ah, yeah.
- I know.

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:33.120
Then you pumped them out

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.160
and you're like, "Wait a
minute, you're fantastic."

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:35.993
Yeah.
- No,

00:01:35.993 --> 00:01:37.320
she's completely the best.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.900
But I always was like,
you know, the first,

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.240
I would say like, the
first year was really hard

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:43.073
and that's when I wrote

00:01:43.073 --> 00:01:44.257
that show.
- Yeah.

00:01:44.257 --> 00:01:46.260
- It was just like this
period where I was,

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.390
like I felt as the dad.

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:50.520
Like, I was like nothing.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:52.860
You know, like I was the
intern of the family.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.400
Like, I was this pudgy
milkless vice president

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:57.720
of the family.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:01.890
Huge title, no power,
also oversees Congress.

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:04.740
And I was just like, this is so hard.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:09.090
And then, but now, she's eight
and she's just the greatest.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:10.170
And I, it's...

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:11.190
I mean, it's amazing.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:12.810
- You're gonna have another special

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:14.370
about when she's a teenager. (chuckles)

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:15.203
- Oh yeah.

00:02:15.203 --> 00:02:16.170
No, I mean, I...

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:18.780
My brain goes to when she's 16,

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:21.600
'cause she's eight now, but
my brain goes to when she's 16

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.910
and she's gonna be like,
"My dad is garbage."

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:24.900
You know what I mean?
- Yeah.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:26.580
- And it'll happen.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:27.480
Like that's natural.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.220
That's what development is.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:32.730
But like my, you know, my dad
didn't have to deal with that.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:35.227
Like it was the '80s, no
one listened to children.

00:02:35.227 --> 00:02:36.060
(all laughing)

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:39.842
Like we said it, but he was
like, "Is someone talking?"

00:02:39.842 --> 00:02:40.675
You know what I mean?
- Sure.

00:02:40.675 --> 00:02:42.570
- And that's how I ended
up being a comedian.

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:45.540
But the...
(all laughing)

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:46.890
- Childhood trauma.
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:47.850
- For the win.
- But like,

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.010
when she's 16, she'll be
like, "My dad is garbage."

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:52.002
And I'm gonna be like, "She's so right."

00:02:52.002 --> 00:02:52.835
(all laughing)

00:02:52.835 --> 00:02:55.710
How can I amplify her
voice? She's so brave.

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:56.760
You know what I mean?

00:02:58.356 --> 00:03:01.020
- (laughs) How can I amplify her voice?

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.120
- She's the great, I mean...

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:03.953
I'm so lucky.

00:03:03.953 --> 00:03:04.973
She's the greatest.
- Oh, my God.

00:03:04.973 --> 00:03:07.140
You are so funny.
- Oh.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.567
- Oh, you've covered
so many areas already.

00:03:09.567 --> 00:03:11.220
- (laughs) Yeah.
- Okay, so wait.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:12.053
Your Broadway show,

00:03:12.053 --> 00:03:13.257
you're talking about "The
Old Man and the Pool."

00:03:13.257 --> 00:03:14.090
- Yes.
- And now,

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:15.065
you made the jump to Netflix.

00:03:15.065 --> 00:03:16.560
You were so successful that they...

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:17.580
You now are on Netflix.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:18.660
- It's now on Netflix.
- That's amazing. Yeah.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:19.890
It's a...

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:22.140
I'm so excited for two reasons.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:23.850
One is that it, you know,

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:26.550
the show is about death and mortality.

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:29.610
And I set out like four
years ago to write this show.

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:32.880
That's all essentially like jokes

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:36.090
and stories about life and
death and losing people

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:37.230
and how hard that is

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:40.260
and how can we make
people laugh from that.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:41.490
Like, can I do that?

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:43.350
And so I had this fear when I did it,

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:46.950
like, "Oh no, like what if
this is really hard for people

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:48.180
to hear who are going

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:49.770
through losing people and all that stuff?"

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:51.600
And actually it was the opposite.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:52.433
- Yeah.
It was this thing

00:03:52.433 --> 00:03:54.874
where people would come up to
me after shows and be like,

00:03:54.874 --> 00:03:56.280
you know, "I lost my dad.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:57.570
I lost someone in my family recently

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.370
and that's what I needed
to hear right now.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:00.990
Like, I needed to laugh.
It was hard for me

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:02.370
to laugh for a long time."

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:03.698
- Yeah.
- So that was cool.

00:04:03.698 --> 00:04:04.531
- The connection of that. Yeah.

00:04:04.531 --> 00:04:05.364
- And then the other side of it is like,

00:04:05.364 --> 00:04:07.330
and I was gonna ask you folks, this is...

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:09.780
I talked about this in the show.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.640
We weren't an I love
you family growing up.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:12.570
Like we don't say I love you.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:15.750
Like, in our family we say, "Take care,"

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:18.370
which is not the same,
- Which is kind of like,

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:19.203
which is kind of like

00:04:19.203 --> 00:04:20.953
what the person says at the grocery store.

00:04:20.953 --> 00:04:22.710
Like when you see them
and like, "So take care."

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:25.200
- It's a very grocery store,
bank teller kind of thing.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:26.460
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:28.920
- And it's kind of a
passive-aggressive command.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:30.000
Like-
- It's a little removed.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.200
- Yeah, like, I'm gonna need you

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:32.910
to do something for me, take care.

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:33.870
You know what I mean?
(all laughing)

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:37.143
And did you or were you
I love you families?

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:40.590
- We were, and I mean, and we are.

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:41.423
- Oh, that's sweet.

00:04:41.423 --> 00:04:44.520
- I mean, I say it many,
many times to my kids a day.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:45.660
- Oh, no, no.
- It's in Illinois.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:46.920
- I do it to my daughter.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:47.753
Oh, yeah, sure.

00:04:47.753 --> 00:04:48.600
I'm saying-
- I was gonna say-

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.010
- I'm saying with my folks. (chuckles)

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:52.110
- Yeah. They were, they were little.

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:53.100
- That's sweet.
- Yeah.

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:54.610
- You know, the thing was, yeah,

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:56.190
like, it was weird because I, you know,

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:58.890
I'd be dodging, you know,

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:01.950
I'd be dodging smacks
across the head one moment.

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:02.783
- Yeah.
- And then,

00:05:02.783 --> 00:05:03.616
I love you would come as,

00:05:03.616 --> 00:05:05.264
"Do you?"
- Yeah.

00:05:05.264 --> 00:05:06.200
(all laughing)

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:07.331
- Then you're like,
- So it was a little,

00:05:07.331 --> 00:05:08.430
- And then you're like,
- It was a little.

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:09.263
- You were-
- You know-

00:05:09.263 --> 00:05:10.830
- For the next 20 years, you work on

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:12.390
what the definition of love is.

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:13.223
- Yeah.

00:05:13.223 --> 00:05:15.013
Plausible deniability after that.

00:05:15.013 --> 00:05:17.400
- Yeah, bro.
- That's incredible.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.641
That's any kid in the south.

00:05:19.641 --> 00:05:20.580
(all laughing)

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:22.410
Let's do another commercial break.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:24.247
Mike's latest standup special is called

00:05:24.247 --> 00:05:25.260
"The Old Man and the Pool."

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:27.903
You can see it on Netflix
starting November 21st.

